use strict;
use warnings;

# fix lib paths, some may be relative
BEGIN { # BEGIN RT CMD BOILERPLATE
    require File::Spec;
    require Cwd;
    my @libs = ("@RT_LIB_PATH@", "@LOCAL_LIB_PATH@");
    my $bin_path;

    for my $lib (@libs) {
        unless ( File::Spec->file_name_is_absolute($lib) ) {
            $bin_path ||= ( File::Spec->splitpath(Cwd::abs_path(__FILE__)) )[1];
            $lib = File::Spec->catfile( $bin_path, File::Spec->updir, $lib );
        }
        unshift @INC, $lib;
    }


}

use RT::Interface::CLI qw(Init);

my %opt = ( 'dry-run' => 1 );
Init( \%opt, 'old-key-file=s', 'dry-run!' );

Pod::Usage::pod2usage( -message => "--old-key-file is required\n", -exitval => 2 )
    unless $opt{'old-key-file'};

my $old_key = ReadKey( $opt{'old-key-file'} );
my $new_key = RT->Config->Get('SecretKey') // '';

if ( $old_key eq $new_key ) {
    print STDERR "The old key matches the configured \$SecretKey. Set the new key in RT_SiteConfig.pm first.\n";
    exit 2;
}

my $users = RT::Users->new( RT->SystemUser );
$users->FindAllRows;
$users->Limit( FIELD => 'TOTPSecret', OPERATOR => 'STARTSWITH', VALUE => 'gcm:' );

my ( $rotated, $already, @failed ) = ( 0, 0 );

$RT::Handle->BeginTransaction unless $opt{'dry-run'};

while ( my $user = $users->Next ) {
    my $raw   = $user->__Value('TOTPSecret');
    my $plain = $user->_DecryptTOTPSecret( $raw, $old_key );

    unless ( length $plain ) {
        if ( length $user->_DecryptTOTPSecret( $raw, $new_key ) ) {
            $already++;
        }
        else {
            push @failed, $user;
        }
        next;
    }

    unless ( $opt{'dry-run'} ) {
        # SetTOTPSecret would record a transaction showing a change that
        # never happened; the secret itself is unchanged.
        my ( $ok, $msg ) = $user->_Set(
            Field             => 'TOTPSecret',
            Value             => $user->_EncryptTOTPSecret($plain),
            RecordTransaction => 0,
        );
        unless ($ok) {
            $RT::Handle->Rollback;
            printf STDERR "Unable to update user #%d (%s): %s\nRolled back; nothing changed.\n",
                $user->Id, $user->Name, $msg;
            exit 1;
        }
    }
    $rotated++;
}

$RT::Handle->Commit unless $opt{'dry-run'};

printf "%s %d TOTP secret(s); %d already used the new key; %d could not be decrypted.\n",
    ( $opt{'dry-run'} ? 'Would re-encrypt' : 'Re-encrypted' ), $rotated, $already, scalar @failed;
printf "  #%d %s\n", $_->Id, $_->Name for @failed;
print "Dry run; rerun with --no-dry-run to save changes.\n" if $opt{'dry-run'};

exit( @failed ? 1 : 0 );

sub ReadKey {
    my $file = shift;
    open my $fh, '<', $file or die "Unable to read $file: $!\n";
    my $key = do { local $/; <$fh> };
    close $fh;
    $key =~ s/\r?\n\z// if defined $key;
    die "$file is empty\n" unless defined $key && length $key;
    return $key;
}

__END__

=head1 NAME

rt-rotate-secret-key - Re-encrypt stored TOTP secrets under a new $SecretKey

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    rt-rotate-secret-key --old-key-file /root/old-secret-key [--no-dry-run]

=head1 DESCRIPTION

RT encrypts each user's TOTP MFA secret with a key derived from
C<$SecretKey>. Changing C<$SecretKey> leaves those secrets undecryptable,
and every enrolled user must enroll again. This script decrypts each
stored secret with the old key and re-encrypts it with the C<$SecretKey>
now set in F<RT_SiteConfig.pm>, so users keep their existing
authenticator entries.

By default the script runs in dry-run mode: it reports what it would do
and changes nothing. Pass C<--no-dry-run> to save the changes.

Secrets that already decrypt with the new key are skipped, so the script
is safe to rerun. Users whose secret decrypts with neither key are
listed and left untouched; reset their MFA enrollment from the user
admin page. The script exits 1 if any user could not be decrypted.

The script records no transactions, but it does update C<LastUpdated> on
each user it re-encrypts.

Changing C<$SecretKey> also invalidates outstanding password-reset and
MFA-reset links; this script can't preserve them. See "Changing the
Secret Key" in F<docs/authentication.pod> for everything a change affects.

=head1 OPTIONS

=over

=item C<--old-key-file> I<path>

Required. A file containing the old key, the value RT used before the
change. The script reads the key from a file rather than the command
line so it doesn't appear in the process list or shell history.

The file must contain only the key itself: not the C<Set(...)> line,
and without surrounding quotes. A single trailing newline is ignored;
any other whitespace counts as part of the key. For example, if
F<RT_SiteConfig.pm> had:

    Set($SecretKey, 'some-random-string');

the file contains:

    some-random-string

If the value was written in double quotes with escapes such as C<\$>,
write the string Perl produced, without the escapes.

If C<$SecretKey> was empty or unset, RT used C<$DatabasePassword> as the
key, so put the C<$DatabasePassword> value in the file. If you changed
C<$DatabasePassword> while C<$SecretKey> was empty, use the old
database password.

=item C<--no-dry-run>

Save the changes. Without it, the script reports what it would do and
changes nothing.

=back

The new key is not an option. The script uses the C<$SecretKey> in RT's
configuration files, so set the new key there before running it. If the
configured key matches the old key file, the script stops without
checking any users.

=head1 PROCEDURE

Run these steps as the user who normally runs RT's F<sbin> tools; the
script reads RT's configuration and connects to its database.

=over

=item 1.

Create the old key file, readable only by you. To keep the key out of
your shell history, paste it into C<cat> and press Ctrl-D:

    (umask 077; cat > /root/old-secret-key)

=item 2.

Stop RT, including the web server, mail gateway, and cron jobs, so no
one enrolls or verifies MFA under the wrong key.

=item 3.

Set the new C<$SecretKey> in F<RT_SiteConfig.pm>. C<openssl rand -base64 48>
produces a suitable value.

=item 4.

Do a dry run:

    rt-rotate-secret-key --old-key-file /root/old-secret-key

It prints a summary such as:

    Would re-encrypt 42 TOTP secret(s); 0 already used the new key; 0 could not be decrypted.
    Dry run; rerun with --no-dry-run to save changes.

If it lists every enrolled user as undecryptable, the old key file is
wrong. Check it for quotes, escapes, or stray whitespace, and run the dry
run again.

=item 5.

Save the changes:

    rt-rotate-secret-key --old-key-file /root/old-secret-key --no-dry-run

=item 6.

Start RT and delete the old key file.

=back

=cut
